4. LOSS MODELLING (score-driven Monte Carlo)
==============================================================================
   Simulation: 100,000 simulated years, score-driven frequencies,
   Student-t dependence, catastrophe years enabled.

   Expected Annual Loss (EAL)    : $5.45M
   1-in-100 year loss (P99.0)    : $45.57M
   1-in-200 year loss (P99.5)    : $59.79M
   1-in-1000 year loss (P99.9)   : $110.17M
   Expected Shortfall 99% (ES99) : $72.30M
   P(no loss year)               : 14.7%

   Scenario contribution to EAL:
     ransomware         1.11M  (20.3%)
     breach             0.89M  (16.3%)
     cloud_outage       0.88M  (16.1%)
     bec                0.83M  (15.2%)
     supply_chain       0.79M  (14.5%)
     bi                 0.52M  ( 9.6%)
     ot_physical        0.43M  ( 7.9%)

==============================================================================
5. VAR / EXPECTED SHORTFALL INTERPRETATION
==============================================================================
   VaR 99%    $45.57M: in 99 of 100 years, the loss stays below this.
   ES 99%     $72.30M: IF a 1-in-100 year occurs, the AVERAGE loss
              in that tail is this much -- the truly decision-relevant number,
              because it captures how bad the bad years really get.

   ES99 / EAL = 13.3x: the tail is 13.3 times the
   average year.  This is the 'catastrophe multiplier' -- a single 1-in-100
   event can be worth over a decade of average losses.

